  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "Solve the linear congruence $3x≡4\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7)$ using trial and error. Express your answer as a nonnegative integer smaller than $7.$\n\n$x≡$\n{{field-1}}$\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7)$",
  "worked_solution": "Since the modulus $n=7$ is fairly small, we can solve the congruence by trial and error. \n\nSubstituting $x=0,1,2,3,4,5,6$ into the congruence, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\cdot 0 & ≡0≢4 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,7) &  & \\times  \\\\ 3\\cdot 1 & ≡3≢4 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,7) &  & \\times  \\\\ 3\\cdot 2 & ≡6≢4 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,7) &  & \\times  \\\\ 3\\cdot 3 & ≡9≡7+2≡2≢4 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,7) &  & \\times  \\\\ 3\\cdot 4 & ≡12≡7+5≡5≢4 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,7) &  & \\times  \\\\ 3\\cdot 5 & ≡15≡2\\cdot 7+1≡1≢4 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,7) &  & \\times  \\\\ 3\\cdot 6 & ≡18≡2\\cdot 7+4≡4 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,7) &  & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x≡6\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7).$\n\n**Note**: Any number congruent to $6$ modulo $7$ is a solution. For example, note that $13≡6,$ and this also satisfies our congruence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x & ≡3\\cdot 13\\, &  & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡39\\, &  & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡5\\cdot 7+4\\, &  & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡0+4\\, &  & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡4\\,\\,✓\\, &  & (\\text{mod}\\,7)\\end{aligned}\n$$",
